findings.body:
In `handleJSONSchemaChange`, the success path ends with `return True` (capital T). JavaScript and TypeScript have no `True` binding, so `tsc` and the frontend lint will fail on this file.

At runtime the state update still happens because `handlePayloadChange('json_schema')(value)` runs first. The `ReferenceError` is then caught by the `catch` below, which returns `null`. The code only works because the error is swallowed. The intended return value should be `true`, or the return should be removed if the editor's `onChange` does not use it.

::diff{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="130-145"}

::suggestion{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="137-140"}
      const v = JSON.parse(value)
      handlePayloadChange('json_schema')(value)
      return true
    }

`handleJSONSchemaChange` ignores invalid input: its `catch` returns `null` without calling `handlePayloadChange`. So `tempPayload.json_schema` keeps the last valid value. If the user edits the schema into invalid JSON and clicks Save, `handleConfirm` validates and saves the old schema. The user's in-progress edit is dropped with no error.

Suggested direction: track the raw editor text (or an error flag) in component state. In `handleConfirm`, block the save with the existing `jsonSchemaInvalid` toast when that text fails to parse. Do not rely on `tempPayload.json_schema`, which only holds the last valid value.

::diff{file="web/app/components/app/configuration/config-var/config-modal/index.tsx" lines="130-145"}
